-- Version 9 → 10: sprints.
--
-- One new table and two nullable columns. See Sprints.sq for what a sprint is,
-- Issues.sq's sprint_id for why that reference is single-column, and Projects.sq's
-- active_sprint_id for why "which sprint is active" is a column on the project
-- rather than a flag on the sprint.
--
-- ── Nothing to back-fill, and that is the whole design ──────────────────────
--
-- Unlike 4.sqm, which had to seed a status row into every project that predated
-- it, this file seeds nothing. It does not create a first sprint, a "Sprint 1",
-- or a synthetic backlog sprint for anybody.
--
-- That is not laziness about the deployed volume — it is the property the
-- feature rests on. The backlog is `sprint_id IS NULL`, an absence rather than a
-- row, so a project with zero sprints has an empty sprint list, no board control,
-- and every issue correctly un-scheduled. Seeding a backlog sprint into every
-- project would give pure-kanban boards a sprint axis they never asked for and
-- turn "invisible by absence" into "invisible by feature flag", which is a
-- different and worse design. The state after this migration runs is therefore
-- indistinguishable from the state before it for every project that exists —
-- 7.sqm's situation, and for a related reason.
--
-- That is the opposite of its immediate neighbour: 8.sqm adds a `position` to
-- every label and component and must back-fill each one, because the column is
-- NOT NULL and a constant default would replace alphabetical order with an
-- arbitrary one. The difference is that 8.sqm changes something every project
-- already has, and this adds something no project has yet.
--
-- ── Why plain ALTER TABLE, when 2.sqm's sibling had to rebuild ──────────────
--
-- Both new columns are single-column, nullable references, which is precisely
-- the case SQLite's ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN accepts — the same ground 3.sqm
-- covered for assignee_id, and its comment is the long version of this one. The
-- default is omitted rather than stated because SQLite REQUIRES the default of
-- an added column with a REFERENCES clause to be NULL.
--
-- issues.sprint_id could not have been rebuilt into a composite key instead even
-- if a rebuild were free, and Issues.sq says why: composite and ON DELETE SET
-- NULL are mutually exclusive here, because SET NULL would null project_id too.
--
-- ── Order matters ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- `sprints` is created first because both ALTERs point at it. SQLite resolves a
-- REFERENCES target lazily and would accept them in either order on a database
-- with foreign keys off — which is how Database.migrateSchema runs — but a file
-- that only works with the constraints disabled is a trap for whoever edits it
-- next.
--
-- The CREATE below is duplicated from Sprints.sq minus its prose, and must stay
-- in step with it. MigrationTest is the enforcement: it applies every .sqm to
-- the previous snapshot and compares the result column for column and foreign
-- key for foreign key against what the .sq files create from scratch.

CREATE TABLE sprints (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
    project_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
    name TEXT NOT NULL COLLATE NOCASE,
    position INTEGER NOT NULL,
    completed_at INTEGER,

    FOREIGN KEY (project_id) REFERENCES projects(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    UNIQUE (project_id, name),
    UNIQUE (id, project_id)
);

ALTER TABLE issues ADD COLUMN sprint_id INTEGER REFERENCES sprints(id) ON DELETE SET NULL;

ALTER TABLE projects ADD COLUMN active_sprint_id INTEGER REFERENCES sprints(id) ON DELETE SET NULL;
